{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power","id":1037937,"title":"Nuclear Power","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-01-25T11:35:27-08:00","id":1037937,"title":"Nuclear Power","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=BEpd6zA2TO4\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=BEpd6zA2TO4\" target=\"_blank\"> 404 Not Found </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=BEpd6zA2TO4\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=BEpd6zA2TO4 </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> One of my greatest shames when I was younger is that I was antinuclear power, I helped in a small way to contribute to the Luddites who have in there own way done as much damage as the fossil fuel industry to the environment. <br /> <br /> So I have decided to start a thread on this issue in the hopes that other will not make the same mistakes that I have.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:51:31-08:00","id":1037946,"title":"","content":"I'm not anti-nuclear the way I am anti-coal or anti-oil, but I feel like the time for nuclear power has passed. I expect renewable energy to outstrip all other power-generating technologies in the next few decades.<br /> <br /> Which is not to say I would object to a nuclear plant being built to specifically replace some number of coal-fired plants, but I'd rather see wind or solar plants instead.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T11:57:52-08:00","id":1037950,"title":"","content":"Responsibly implemented there's nothing wrong with nuclear energy and I think it should be better utilized. The problem, of course, is that it needs to be <i>responsibly</i> implemented and unfortunately reality has shown that mistakes can be made. A mistake at a nuclear power plant can have much larger and much more permanent effects than a mistake at a coal plant.<br /> <br /> I'm in general agreement with semicasual that while I don't necessarily have a problem with nuclear plants, per se, I'd definitely rather see wind, solar, hydro and geothermal plants being built instead of nuclear ones simply because they don't bring with them the potential problems that a nuclear power plant does.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:01:47-08:00","id":1037954,"title":"","content":"People here are incredibly scared of Nuclear Energy due to living in the shadow of Chernobyl, which is very funny, because we are so coal based that we lose tons of people due to bad air.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:07:25-08:00","id":1037960,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=cYABUl-fh8Y\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=cYABUl-fh8Y\" target=\"_blank\"> 404 Not Found </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=cYABUl-fh8Y\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=cYABUl-fh8Y </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:08:12-08:00","id":1037961,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1037954#post1037954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">People here are incredibly scared of Nuclear Energy due to living in the shadow of Chernobyl, which is very funny, because we are so coal based that we lose tons of people due to bad air.</div> </div> </div> </div> Actually coal ash is more radioactive then nuclear waste. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.google.ca/url?sa=t&amp;source=web&amp;rct=j&amp;url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/coal-ash-is-more-radioactive-than-nuclear-waste/&amp;ved=0ahUKEwi18uPjjN7RAhVG5oMKHUvYD3AQFggaMAA&amp;usg=AFQjCNHVKfTjG2tlZf3K3LzWv3FjiTOy_g&amp;sig2=9Ac-yGV5bkndWqF2oByqaw\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.google.ca/url?sa=t&amp;source=web&amp;rct=j&amp;url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/coal-ash-is-more-radioactive-than-nuclear-waste/&amp;ved=0ahUKEwi18uPjjN7RAhVG5oMKHUvYD3AQFggaMAA&amp;usg=AFQjCNHVKfTjG2tlZf3K3LzWv3FjiTOy_g&amp;sig2=9Ac-yGV5bkndWqF2oByqaw\" target=\"_blank\"> Redirect Notice </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.google.ca/url?sa=t&amp;source=web&amp;rct=j&amp;url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/coal-ash-is-more-radioactive-than-nuclear-waste/&amp;ved=0ahUKEwi18uPjjN7RAhVG5oMKHUvYD3AQFggaMAA&amp;usg=AFQjCNHVKfTjG2tlZf3K3LzWv3FjiTOy_g&amp;sig2=9Ac-yGV5bkndWqF2oByqaw\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.google.ca/url?sa=t&amp;source=web&amp;rct=j&amp;url=https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/coal-ash-is-more-radioactive-than-nuclear-waste/&amp;ved=0ahUKEwi18uPjjN7RAhVG5oMKHUvYD3AQFggaMAA&amp;usg=AFQjCNHVKfTjG2tlZf3K3LzWv3FjiTOy_g&amp;sig2=9Ac-yGV5bkndWqF2oByqaw </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> It's ironic that a side effect of fighting nuclear power is more radioactive waste, not less.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2241,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","user":"Omegaphallic","date":". 01-25-2017, 03:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T12:37:58-08:00","id":1037985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=bnMla02DcRc\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=bnMla02DcRc\" target=\"_blank\"> 404 Not Found </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=bnMla02DcRc\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=bnMla02DcRc </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T14:12:11-08:00","id":1038035,"title":"","content":"I'll add to the voices of generally OK with it, but the money spent prevent disasters seems difficult to justify as renewable technology is at the point where economic and political interests are the problem with implementation.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1037954#post1037954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">People here are incredibly scared of Nuclear Energy due to living in the shadow of Chernobyl, which is very funny, because we are so coal based that we lose tons of people due to bad air.</div> </div> </div> </div>I have a good family friend that moved to the US from Ukraine and was actually one of the engineers that worked to contain the Cernobyl event. He still can't quite understand how I voluntarily live in one of the US's major coal areas <b>and</b> within the fallout range of Three Mile Island.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T18:54:27-08:00","id":1038172,"title":"","content":"I absolutely support renewables, but it might not be the right solution for every location. <br /> <br /> And if it wasn't for the anti nuke lobby, climate change might have been licked decades ago.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T20:13:50-08:00","id":1038231,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1038035#post1038035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll add to the voices of generally OK with it, but the money spent prevent disasters seems difficult to justify as renewable technology is at the point where economic and political interests are the problem with implementation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I have a good family friend that moved to the US from Ukraine and was actually one of the engineers that worked to contain the Cernobyl event. He still can't quite understand how I voluntarily live in one of the US's major coal areas <b>and</b> within the fallout range of Three Mile Island.</div> </div> </div> </div> I actually here the Three Mile Island is beautiful very green place now, because the radiation drove the people away. Think about that for a minute, nuclear fallout it less destructive on green spaces then then human habitation.<br /> <br /> Don't get me wrong I think humanity is worth the sacrifice, I'm just surprised more ecoterrorist groups don't see that and think maybe dirty bombs are the lesser evil (I don't condone dirty bombs, they are morally wrong, I just mean from their perspective I could see the temptation).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-25T20:17:11-08:00","id":1038235,"title":"","content":"While no single renewable is right for every location, most locations are good for one of them. As well, there needs to be a much better (and more decentralized) power grid; which is actually part of the biggest problem right now.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that nuclear plants aren't good for everywhere either, and there were some very reasonable concerns for a long time that have only really been addressed in the past 15 years technologically speaking; mostly dealing with waste disposal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T05:22:19-08:00","id":1038350,"title":"","content":"It would also help to have more effient transmission cables, people are working on that technology.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T10:01:03-08:00","id":1038460,"title":"","content":"Yeah, nuclear energy is one those things we could do more with embracing. It's not a permanent fix, but god damn if it doesn't make for a hell of a stop gap.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T10:28:41-08:00","id":1038474,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power#post1037937\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=BEpd6zA2TO4\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/shared?ci=BEpd6zA2TO4</a><br /> <br /> One of my greatest shames when I was younger is that I was antinuclear power, I helped in a small way to contribute to the Luddites who have in there own way done as much damage as the fossil fuel industry to the environment.<br /> <br /> So I have decided to start a thread on this issue in the hopes that other will not make the same mistakes that I have.</div> </div> </div> </div>But Phallic now that are forms of energy that have no risk of hurtying anyone , why would one pick nuclear energy over wind energy for instance? Especially since wind energy leaves no waste","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T10:46:28-08:00","id":1038485,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1038474#post1038474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But Phallic now that are forms of energy that have no risk of hurtying anyone , why would one pick nuclear energy over wind energy for instance? Especially since wind energy leaves no waste</div> </div> </div> </div>The challenge of relying heavily on wind energy is storage and distribution. Honestly storage and distribution are the two biggest engineering hurdles facing most forms of renewables (though they're steadily getting better). Nuclear power has these mostly solved, in addition to having a more favorable input/output ratio. Its challenges are a combination of public perception and large up front costs that take a long time to break even.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T11:06:32-08:00","id":1038490,"title":"","content":"Solar and wind power require a lot of land space, and wind turbines harm birds. They're also dependent on weather patterns. I love to see more clean power being generated, but solar and wind are still far from perfect from an environmental perspective.<br /> <br /> I would like to see more investment in, and studies done on, liquid-fluoride thorium reactors. Thorium has a significantly higher melting point than uranium (the most common nuclear fuel) which not only reduces the risk of meltdown in and of itself but allows for the fuel chamber to be designed with a plug that melts before the thorium does--in the event that the reactor gets too hot, the plug melts, the fuel drains and the heat dissipates. Here's a diagram:<br /> <br /> <img src=\"http://illumin.usc.edu/assets/media/1067/thorium_reactor04.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/233-daydreamer","name":"Daydreamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar233_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"193"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T12:12:10-08:00","id":1038516,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Daydreamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1038490#post1038490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...wind turbines harm birds...</div> </div> </div> </div>...not nearly as much as <a href=\"https://www.sciencenews.org/article/cats-kill-more-one-billion-birds-each-year\" target=\"_blank\">domestic cats</a> or <a href=\"http://www.livescience.com/19908-migratory-birds-killed-towers.html\" target=\"_blank\">radio towers</a>, but I don't hear anyone proposing to get rid of those.<br /> <br /> I agree with the rest of your post generally, but it gets my goat whenever anyone trots out &quot;but think of the BIRDS!!!&quot; as a complaint about wind power. It's kind of like how Omegaphallic brought up how coal-ash is actually more radioactive than than the radiation generated by a nuclear plant earlier in the thread - yes, it's true, but is the difference big enough to be worth caring about? I think you'll live longer by looking both ways before crossing the street and making sure to eat healthy than you will by fixating on minute sources of radiation in your environment. And if you want to save the birds, reducing overall environmental pollution from fossil fuels will have more impact than banning turbines.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 01-26-2017, 06:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T13:13:00-08:00","id":1038548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Caitiff Primogen</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1038485#post1038485\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Nuclear power has these mostly solved, in addition to having a more favorable input/output ratio. Its challenges are a combination of public perception and large up front costs that take a long time to break even.</div> </div> </div> </div>The second half here is really important to remember if you're considering nuclear as a bridge to purely renewable sources.<br /> <br /> Also, lets remember that wind and solar are not the only renewables out there. Iceland, for example, uses renewable geothermal for a massive amount of their energy consumption. Hydroelectric has environmental concerns with habitat disruption, but can also be coupled with water storage concerns. Etc.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-26T18:04:27-08:00","id":1038665,"title":"","content":"A lot of the nuke power plant tech has to do with waste, and potential waste products, and the rest tends to be the lack of improvement due to multiple reasons...<br /> <br /> Bear with me, Nuclear energy, the north american power grid and alternative energy systems are something of a hobby horse topic of mine.<br /> <br /> One of the big reasons the majority of currently used plants in the US generate so much radioactive waste is the specific designs chosen, in large part due to political concerns over the years. See there are a lot of more efficient designs, but most of them (including most of the low-pressure molten salt reactors) have the *potential* to theoretically result in material that could be utilized in weapons... There was a lot of political concern about un-monitored weapon production many decades ago. So there's a drive to stay with the proven no-legal-headaches designs. That got changed a bit as some laws got tweaked in the Obama administration.<br /> <br /> On the low-pressure liquid thorium (molten salt) reactors they do indeed have a LOT of potential and have since the 60's when they were invented, trialed, and even scoped/evaluated for the power source for military bombers. Unfortunately the ideas while going through an uptick right now, have been rather dormant for quite a while. As a result the problem with them is not a need to do research... we've built research reactors from the designs, even ones that are quite small. There are two catches, first is that they're in that technically-possibly-this-might-be-able-to-make-trace-amounts-of-enriched-weapon-material band... bearing in mind, this is one of those situations where *technically* it happens, and *technically* if someone wanted to go to the effort to make a way to filter out plutonium from the running molten core, they could get material that with a lot of enrichment effort could make a weapon... but it's not like you could turn a tap and it goes kind of thing, it's a handful of atoms transform and exist for a duration measured in hours before they're changed again. The second catch, and by far the bigger one, is that there are no questions about the basic physics or functionality, but there ARE concerns and questions about long term durability, what sort of repair/replacement is needed over a long period of time and that sort of thing. Molten salt is particularly rough on components, but the rest of the system has questions as well. It's not that there's a *problem* with that in itself, but what it means is that the only real way to get solid data on it is to build a commercial scale MSR or ten, study them aggressively for up to 20 years and produce the charts of risks and operational procedures.<br /> <br /> And that's where the problem comes in. Because it's such a high capital investment to get the data, nobody really wants to jump in and start building commercial test plants.<br /> <br /> <br /> And it's unfortunately not just Nuclear power that has those questions raised once the plants start running. The latest big example of that is the Ivanpah* solar power plant. The last time I checked up on it (a year or two ago now I think) they had just significantly increased the amount of natural gas they could burn there for the second time... To the point where if they burned all the natural gas they were allowed to purchase in a somewhat clean modern natural gas power plant it would match the projected output of the solar plant. <br /> <br /> And yeah, there's the lesser problems with wind turbines and birds and bats and so on. Every form of electrical generation has problems. My personal favorite &quot;whoops&quot; example was when they tried to install tidal generators on the bay of Fundy about 7-8 years ago. The turbines lasted I think something like 2 weeks before the blades were torn apart from the tidal forces. Again, a case of the tests all working out, the engineering seeming sound but when it hits commercial production... problems.<br /> <br /> <br /> *-If you're not familiar with it, it is a giant solar power plant. It operates by using mirrors to focus light on a tower to heat a liquid salt material, which is then used to heat water, and do all the normal power plant things. The catch is you need to keep the salt molten and flowing even when there's not light from the focused sunlight to heat it... like all night. A feat they achieve by heating it with natural gas.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-28T12:34:03-08:00","id":1039552,"title":"","content":"In Canada we use Candu reactors, a unique Canadian nuclear technology that was designed to address some uniquely Canada concerns at the time, but the technology has it's advantages.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-28T13:11:48-08:00","id":1039574,"title":"","content":"&quot;CANDU advantages<br /> CANDU stands for CANada Deuterium Uranium, because it was invented in Canada, uses deuterium oxide (also known as heavy water) as a moderator, and uranium as a fuel.<br /> CANDU fuel bundle<br /> CANDU fuel bundle. Source: Nuclear Waste Management Organization.<br /> CANDU reactors are unique in that they use natural, unenriched uranium as a fuel; with some modification, they can also use enriched uranium, mixed fuels, and even thorium. Thus, CANDU reactors are ideally suited for using material from decommissioned nuclear weapons as fuel, helping to reduce global arsenals.<br /> CANDU reactors can be refueled while operating at full power, while most other designs must be shut down for refueling. Moreover, because natural uranium does not require enrichment, fuel costs for CANDU reactors are very low.<br /> CANDU reactors are exceptionally safe. The safety systems are independent from the rest of the plant, and each key safety component has three backups. Not only does this redundancy increase the overall safety of the system, but it also makes it possible to test the safety system while the reactor is operating under full power.&quot;<br /> <br /> I had nearly forgotten CANDU reactors can use Thorium.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-29T21:18:07-08:00","id":1040402,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1039574#post1039574\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;I had nearly forgotten CANDU reactors can use Thorium.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep, we made quite a tidy sum of money selling them around the world several decades ago. Unfortunately they have that same issue I mentioned above in that the design can be used to manufacture weapons grade plutonium (considerably more easily than a thorium MSR)... there was a lot of political tension when we were setting them up in... Turkey I think? Anyway, it's a solid (if a bit old now) design. They are still a high-pressure design with the risks that entails... If a low pressure MSR has failure, even one as bad as say a massive break in the core, pretty much the worst that can happen is the molten core leaks out, cools down and turns to a moderately radioactive lump. Even if someone is actively trying to destroy one, and piles massive amounts of explosives around the core... you're going to get moderately radioactive lumps scattered around, but again... that's about it. Easy to clean up, and the worst-case-accident is able to be cleaned up with a few dump trucks. If a high pressure system has a catastrophic failure you get a lot of lightly radioactive material scattered very far. Fukushima for example.<br /> <br /> But the Candu's are cool. I actually ended up running some rough numbers on them for power plant consumption and output for a World of Darkness style game at one point on them. (Story-teller time mode activated)<br /> <br /> See, one of the things about sea water is that it has uranium in it. Quite a lot. We can filter it out if we want (and have done so in trials) but generally there's no reason to bother because Uranium is not expensive. So I wanted something that would work for a Pentax project... you know the kind, pointlessly evil and environmentally destructive and profit generating for no particularly good reasons. So I settled on Pentex getting the rights to build desalination plants around New York City, doing a whole big publicity launch complete with a water drop corporate mascot. The whole new jobs, more employment, wonderful things for the city, cleaner water for the masses all that jazz. Of course what they *actually* built were completely automated plants that stripped the uranium from the seawater, used it to power reactors (on the same site) and used the power generated to desalinate the water, all in a fully automated system so that nobody but automated security would normally be in any of the plants. Being Pentex of course, they had secondary plans to use the water to slip drugs to the whole of the city. And in order to make sure that the whole of the city was consuming the desalinated water from their plants, well of course the incoming water supply lines to New York City had to have a &quot;structural failure&quot;.<br /> <br /> Still the whole point is that the interesting math I worked out was that with the amount of uranium extracted from seawater as it runs through a desalination plant is about three times the power needed for the most power-hungry of desalination plants.<br /> <br /> Kinda makes me want to see if it would actually be possible to build something like that in real life, an automated power plant and desalination plant that also powers the local town.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T06:19:39-08:00","id":1040612,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1040402#post1040402\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yep, we made quite a tidy sum of money selling them around the world several decades ago. Unfortunately they have that same issue I mentioned above in that the design can be used to manufacture weapons grade plutonium (considerably more easily than a thorium MSR)... there was a lot of political tension when we were setting them up in... Turkey I think? Anyway, it's a solid (if a bit old now) design. They are still a high-pressure design with the risks that entails... If a low pressure MSR has failure, even one as bad as say a massive break in the core, pretty much the worst that can happen is the molten core leaks out, cools down and turns to a moderately radioactive lump. Even if someone is actively trying to destroy one, and piles massive amounts of explosives around the core... you're going to get moderately radioactive lumps scattered around, but again... that's about it. Easy to clean up, and the worst-case-accident is able to be cleaned up with a few dump trucks. If a high pressure system has a catastrophic failure you get a lot of lightly radioactive material scattered very far. Fukushima for example.<br /> <br /> But the Candu's are cool. I actually ended up running some rough numbers on them for power plant consumption and output for a World of Darkness style game at one point on them. (Story-teller time mode activated)<br /> <br /> See, one of the things about sea water is that it has uranium in it. Quite a lot. We can filter it out if we want (and have done so in trials) but generally there's no reason to bother because Uranium is not expensive. So I wanted something that would work for a Pentax project... you know the kind, pointlessly evil and environmentally destructive and profit generating for no particularly good reasons. So I settled on Pentex getting the rights to build desalination plants around New York City, doing a whole big publicity launch complete with a water drop corporate mascot. The whole new jobs, more employment, wonderful things for the city, cleaner water for the masses all that jazz. Of course what they *actually* built were completely automated plants that stripped the uranium from the seawater, used it to power reactors (on the same site) and used the power generated to desalinate the water, all in a fully automated system so that nobody but automated security would normally be in any of the plants. Being Pentex of course, they had secondary plans to use the water to slip drugs to the whole of the city. And in order to make sure that the whole of the city was consuming the desalinated water from their plants, well of course the incoming water supply lines to New York City had to have a &amp;quot;structural failure&amp;quot;.<br /> <br /> Still the whole point is that the interesting math I worked out was that with the amount of uranium extracted from seawater as it runs through a desalination plant is about three times the power needed for the most power-hungry of desalination plants.<br /> <br /> Kinda makes me want to see if it would actually be possible to build something like that in real life, an automated power plant and desalination plant that also powers the local town.</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't know there was uranium in seawater, I now have a whole new way of making anti nuclear advocates shit themselves, thanks! (Kidding)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-30T08:45:27-08:00","id":1040694,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1040612#post1040612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I didn't know there was uranium in seawater, I now have a whole new way of making anti nuclear advocates shit themselves, thanks! (Kidding)</div> </div> </div> </div>The important thing to remember about uranium oxide (how you tend to find it in nature, aka Yellowcake) is that it's mostly harmless. You don't want to breath it in, but that's case with any metal.<br /> <br /> Heck the main use for the stuff a century ago was as a lovely paint colour.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-02T01:42:53-08:00","id":1042282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nicolas Milioni</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1038474#post1038474\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But Phallic now that are forms of energy that have no risk of hurtying anyone , why would one pick nuclear energy over wind energy for instance? Especially since wind energy leaves no waste</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bUm, possibly because commercial nuclear is able to produce base-load, while most renewables emphatically cannot (Geothermal and Large Hydro can, but both are pretty much entirely tapped out at this point).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-02T03:26:23-08:00","id":1042325,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure how you can consider geothermal tapped out at this point considering you have countries like Japan and Canada that have barely touched their best potential geothermal energy sources.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-02T13:24:48-08:00","id":1042567,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Uniform Two Six</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1042282#post1042282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bUm, possibly because commercial nuclear is able to produce base-load, while most renewables emphatically cannot (Geothermal and Large Hydro can, but both are pretty much entirely tapped out at this point).</div> </div> </div> </div>There's also the question of cost vs production capacity. You go nuke, natural gas and coal (generally in that order) when you need a large amount of reliable and cheap power. It's why China has been mass building reactors (to decrease the amount of coal plants). <br /> <br /> Renewable power sources are great, but they're still costing about 3x-10x what a nuke power plant costs, and that's after decades of falling costs. The hope/expectation is that the costs continue to fall until they are more feasible.<br /> <br /> Right now it's not just the cost that renewables are typically having an issue with, but also the production capacity. Unless you're willing to level a city to build solar panels and wind turbines, you're not going to get the power draw of say a boxite smelter. That having been said, lower draw applications (running homes) it's far better at, and when you have distributed small generation you suddenly don't need as much large scale production and can focus it on the industrial uses that DO need it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-02T17:14:08-08:00","id":1042686,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1042325#post1042325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure how you can consider geothermal tapped out at this point considering you have countries like Japan and Canada that have barely touched their best potential geothermal energy sources.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bWhile I'm not an expert, my understanding of Japan is that all of the &quot;potential&quot; geothermal sources remain too technically challenging to capitalize on. I suspect that my understanding is spot-on as Japan is in a dire situation with regard to its power needs. It has effectively no fossil fuel sources of its own (a problem that was the largest single element in the decision to declare war on a nation that had an industrial output <i>eight times </i>its own and an economic output in dollar-value of around <i>45 times </i>its own -- so I doubt that's <i>improved</i> much in the last 76 years), coupled with a societal suspicion of and hatred for nuclear power -- before the Fukushima-Dai-Ichi disaster. If geothermal was that easily accessible, I rather suspect they would have been exploiting the heck out of it by now. Geothermal accounts for less than 1% of the Japanese grid, while the Philippines gets about a fifth of their mix from it.<br /> <br /> \u200bCanada, on the other hand doesn't <i>need</i> geothermal. Most of their power needs are met by hydro. Their population is about 10% that of their southern neighbor, despite having a land mass significantly greater (only Russia is larger), and as one person put it; &quot;Canada has a hydroelectric dam installed just about everywhere one <i>could</i> be installed&quot;.<br /> <br /> \u200bAnd as an aside, when I say that geothermal and hydro are tapped out, I mean here in the States. There may very well be other countries (Iceland for instance) where that does not hold true.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":3506,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","user":"Uniform Two Six","date":". 02-02-2017, 08:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-02T19:14:19-08:00","id":1042747,"title":"","content":"Japan's biggest issue with geothermal is the Onsen lobby, which is fighting geothermal heavily (including publishing a lot of reports about how bad the geothermal power potential in Japan would be) out of fear that a highly valued traditional cultural practice (which makes a huge amount of money) would be at risk from the public not wanting to go to an onsen where there's also a power plant; especially wealthier customers that want to go to an onsen that's away from industry/city life.<br /> <br /> But yes, the US doesn't have much more potential in either source, but we also have things like a lot of space that isn't being put towards economically productive use, which is why there's way more pressure towards wind and solar here.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T04:06:13-08:00","id":1042881,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1042747#post1042747\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...But yes, the US doesn't have much more potential in either source, but we also have things like a lot of space that isn't being put towards economically productive use, which is why there's way more pressure towards wind and solar here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which, to circle back to the statement I was originally responding to, as Large Hydro and Geothermal are largely tapped out leaves Nuclear as the only of the environmentally clean sources able to meet base-load.<br /> <br /> \u200bFrom a practical standpoint, if one is serious about getting off of fossil fuels for commercial electrical power generation, you're going to need a mix which includes Nuclear -- <i>regardless</i> of how much Solar and Wind you can get online.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T07:11:59-08:00","id":1042921,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Uniform Two Six</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1042881#post1042881\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which, to circle back to the statement I was originally responding to, as Large Hydro and Geothermal are largely tapped out leaves Nuclear as the only of the environmentally clean sources able to meet base-load.<br /> <br /> \u200bFrom a practical standpoint, if one is serious about getting off of fossil fuels for commercial electrical power generation, you're going to need a mix which includes Nuclear -- <i>regardless</i> of how much Solar and Wind you can get online.</div> </div> </div> </div>well, and to be honest I think people with money are starting to hold off to see how viable the newest fusion techs are.<br /> <br /> When you're sinking hundreds of millions to billions into a project intended to last decades, the last thing you want to discover is that it's outdated and unable to compete on cost in a decade.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T10:13:10-08:00","id":1043015,"title":"","content":"On the base load issue, while the US doesn't have a lot of future in geo or hydro expansion, we also don't use those power sources nearly as efficiently as possible to make solar and wind more viable. How much we need to expand nuclear would depend a lot on investments in things that aren't power generation.<br /> <br /> Though this gets into the fact that the US power infrastructure is sorely out of date and other such issues that would need to be addressed unless fusion actually becomes a reality in the near future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T12:13:21-08:00","id":1043076,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1042921#post1042921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">well, and to be honest I think people with money are starting to hold off to see how viable the newest fusion techs are.<br /> <br /> When you're sinking hundreds of millions to billions into a project intended to last decades, the last thing you want to discover is that it's outdated and unable to compete on cost in a decade.</div> </div> </div> </div>Don't forget the hype about LENR technology.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T12:22:59-08:00","id":1043083,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Uniform Two Six</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1042686#post1042686\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> \u200bWhile I'm not an expert, my understanding of Japan is that all of the &amp;quot;potential&amp;quot; geothermal sources remain too technically challenging to capitalize on. I suspect that my understanding is spot-on as Japan is in a dire situation with regard to its power needs. It has effectively no fossil fuel sources of its own (a problem that was the largest single element in the decision to declare war on a nation that had an industrial output <i>eight times </i>its own and an economic output in dollar-value of around <i>45 times </i>its own -- so I doubt that's <i>improved</i> much in the last 76 years), coupled with a societal suspicion of and hatred for nuclear power -- before the Fukushima-Dai-Ichi disaster. If geothermal was that easily accessible, I rather suspect they would have been exploiting the heck out of it by now. Geothermal accounts for less than 1% of the Japanese grid, while the Philippines gets about a fifth of their mix from it.<br /> <br /> \u200bCanada, on the other hand doesn't <i>need</i> geothermal. Most of their power needs are met by hydro. Their population is about 10% that of their southern neighbor, despite having a land mass significantly greater (only Russia is larger), and as one person put it; &amp;quot;Canada has a hydroelectric dam installed just about everywhere one <i>could</i> be installed&amp;quot;.<br /> <br /> \u200bAnd as an aside, when I say that geothermal and hydro are tapped out, I mean here in the States. There may very well be other countries (Iceland for instance) where that does not hold true.</div> </div> </div> </div> That's an over simplification of the energy situation in Canada. <br /> <br /> Quebec relies heavily on Hydro power generation, but they do have at least one nuclear power plant. <br /> <br /> Ontario does have some hydro power, but it's uses a lot of nuclear power and fossil fuels as well as renewables. <br /> <br /> Alberta relies mostly on fossil fuels, with some investment in renewables, but the NDP government there is phasing out the coal power plants.<br /> <br /> Those are just some examples.<br /> <br /> So yes, Canada does have some extensive Hydro power resources in some regions, but no where near enough to power Canada by itself.<br /> <br /> Nulcear power is still important to Canada's well being.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-03T16:05:33-08:00","id":1043215,"title":"","content":"Not disagreeing, but the situations that lead Canada to use fossils and nuclear are essentially the same forces that create those scenarios down here in the States. BC has a <i>ton</i> of hydro available, but farther east the potential flattens out (pun intended). As a result, even though there's so much hydro in the west, much of it gets transmitted south to be used in American industrial processes, rather than east to places like Ontario. Theoretically you <i>could</i> do that, but you run into transmission-efficiency issues. Even here in California (which really isn't <i>that</i> big of a place) our transmission loss is about 21% on average. That means that for every five watts of power you produce, only four actually get to the consumer on the other end. Moreover, geothermal isn't really going to help that problem in Canada, since most of the really accessible geothermal sources also (due to geology -- and <i>not</i> coincidentally) are also generally the same regions that have the lion's share of hydro too. Here in California, we're one of the biggest producers of geothermal power in the nation (actually I think we <i>are </i>the biggest, but I don't have the numbers in front of me, so don't quote me on that). This production tends to be from two sources: An area known as &quot;The Geysers&quot; in Northern California (which I don't know all that much about), and the Long Valley Caldera (which I do). In the late 70s, a power company called Southern California Edison sank the first geothermal wells at Diablo Springs in the Caldera and began exporting electricity south. The primary reason that SCE did that had little to do with the accessibility of the geothermal source. Of far greater import was the transmission infrastructure already in the area due to the pre-existing hydro power plants there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T11:25:28-08:00","id":1043648,"title":"","content":"BC's hydro potential is running into major environmental issues.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T12:37:22-08:00","id":1043680,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1043648#post1043648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">BC's hydro potential is running into major environmental issues.</div> </div> </div> </div>And this is really the big key, everything has an environmental cost, and generally they should be considered early in the process of evaluating something. My favourite recent example of this is the desalination plant in southern California. Short version, California, especially the south, gets droughts and is going through a bad one. So someone figured it might be a good idea to invest in a desalination plant, to help make sure people can get water if things get worse and generally in the future. Goes through the standard assessments and everything, gets built and then immediately gets calls (and political pressure) to shut it down. But clean water is a good thing you say, why would anyone demand it get shut down? Because everything has an environmental impact, and one of the things a desalination plant does is dump out a lot of salt, typically back into the ocean... making the water out around the plant more salty. Too salty for some plants/animals to live there.<br /> <br /> Now you can dump it out onto the land, but A: it's a LOT of salt. and B: that's Salting The Earth, ensuring nothing will grow there.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T13:01:48-08:00","id":1043693,"title":"","content":"Depends how deep you bury it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-04T19:07:02-08:00","id":1043805,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1043693#post1043693\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Depends how deep you bury it.</div> </div> </div> </div>True, and trucking vast amounts of salt and waste up hill until you reach a point where you can bury it is indeed an alternative... and an additional environmental impact to consider as well as additional costs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-02-05T22:28:55-08:00","id":1044387,"title":"","content":"Actually there's a more basic problem. I did a story a few years back on a new desalination plant that was commissioned here in California, and got to know some of the engineers at the Alameda County Water District. The only really viable desal plants don't actually use conventional saltwater. They use what hydrological engineers term &quot;brackish water&quot; (which is not what most people think it means). Basically they're pumping mostly fresh water out of an aquifer that has some saltwater intrusion. The reverse-osmosis scheme they use is energy-intensive, so the less salt (and other stuff) they have to filter out, the better. The process does create a stream of fresh water and another of so-called &quot;super-salinated&quot; water, but that latter term is actually highly misleading. The water that is pumped out is actually far less saline than the seawater it's being pumped into.<br /> <br /> \u200bThe desal plant down south that they're putting into service is a true desalination plant using seawater, but the problem is the amount of electricity it takes to remove salt from it in that concentration (a lesser concern -- though still technically challenging and expensive is the wear and tear on the filters themselves, which have a relatively short lifespan in that caustic environment). Basically, it turns out that it is far more economically viable to pump water 500 or so miles from Northern California -- which is exactly what is now underway with an ambitious plan for a <i>third</i> aquaduct through the California Central Valley (including a roughly 70 mile tunnel being planned through the northern Coast Range).<br /> <br /> \u200bAlso, the drought cycle in the state is often a decade or more, meaning that the desal plant will have to be maintained through years and years and only be actually utilized during the most severe periods of drought.<br /> <br /> \u200bI'm frankly astonished that that abortion of a project got funded at all considering how much of a goof-ball idea it was on the conceptual level.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-09T07:29:47-08:00","id":1060512,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1042921#post1042921\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">stuff.</div> </div> </div> </div>Kinda unrelated, but as a graduate of nuclear technologies (among other things), I want to virtual high-five another sensible and well-explains nuclear person <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5238-herbert_west","name":"Herbert_West","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5238_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"473"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-09T12:13:45-08:00","id":1060595,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Odd_Canuck</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1043680#post1043680\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now you can dump it out onto the land, but A: it's a LOT of salt. and B: that's Salting The Earth, ensuring nothing will grow there.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bIn the meantime you have the Northeast states that need salt for the roads during the winter months but keep running low on it. Maybe someone should find a way to get the appropriate officials and businesses in touch with each other and devise a way to turn one state's problem into another's benefit. Seems like a business opportunity for the person who figures out how to solve the logistics and turn a profit.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Onyx Path Forum Administrator</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">Posts in this color are moderator posts</span></b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\"><span style=\"color:#00FF00\">Posts in this color mean a Great Old One has driven me mad.</span></span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11-darksider","name":"Darksider","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11_2.jpeg","title":"Forum Administrator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1692"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-03-09T12:43:49-08:00","id":1060609,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Darksider</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/1037937-nuclear-power?p=1060595#post1060595\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">\u200bIn the meantime you have the Northeast states that need salt for the roads during the winter months but keep running low on it. Maybe someone should find a way to get the appropriate officials and businesses in touch with each other and devise a way to turn one state's problem into another's benefit. Seems like a business opportunity for the person who figures out how to solve the logistics and turn a profit.</div> </div> </div> </div>I suspect that's more of a demand prediction/planning issue than a cost or supply issue. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> And generally (at least in Canada) we've been moving away from classic salts to other materials as much as possible due to the environmental impact. But yeah, that's capitalism. Find someone that has a use for your waste products and is willing to buy them. That's how we ended up with fake logs for the fireplace(surplus wood scraps) and SPAM(mixture of meats too lean for bacon and not large enough for ham)... heck, that's how we ended up with modern asprin (a lot of coal tar and people looking for uses for the stuff)<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Odd_Canuck is not a topical medication or food product and is not to be taken internally or seriously.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/31-odd_canuck","name":"Odd_Canuck","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar31_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1474"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}